E('The gauge length for the anneal sweep of **Table 2** was 127 mm and the crosshead speed 0.25 in min⁻¹, giving a nominal strain rate of 8.3 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹; engineering strain is reported throughout as crosshead extension divided by that gauge length, without extensometry, so the elastic portion of each curve carries the compliance of the load train and the moduli in **Table 2** are apparent rather than true. This test reveals the material\'s super-elasticity. Three specimens were tested for each heat-treatment condition; every value in **Table 2** is the mean of three measurements and the quoted uncertainty is one standard deviation over those three specimens. Replicate specimens were also run for the abnormal-grain-growth treatments, and are discussed where those results are presented.',
  'Engineering strain is reported throughout as crosshead extension divided by the gauge length recorded by the instrument for that test, without extensometry, so the elastic portion of each curve carries the compliance of the load train and the moduli in **Table 2** are apparent rather than true. This test reveals the material\'s super-elasticity. The gauge length was not the same for every dataset, and because crosshead strain over a short gauge is dominated by load-train and grip compliance, elongations measured on different gauges are not compared as strains in this paper. The gauge for each dataset is as follows. The anneal sweep of **Table 2** and **Fig. 2**, on 0.36 mm wire, was run at a gauge length of 127 mm (5.000 in) and a crosshead speed of 0.25 in min⁻¹ (nominal strain rate 8.3 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹); three specimens were tested for each heat-treatment condition, every value in **Table 2** is the mean of three measurements, and the quoted uncertainty is one standard deviation over those three specimens. The loading–unloading–heating test of **Fig. 3**, described next, was run on the same 127 mm gauge. The prolonged-anneal wires and the cyclically heat-treated specimens of Sec. 3.5 (**Fig. 10**) were tested at three gauges — 127 mm for the 40- and 80-minute wires and for the ≈1–1.3 mm rod specimens, 6.0 mm (0.236 in) for one further three-cycle rod specimen, and 25.4 mm (1.000 in) for the quartz-tube-cycled 0.36 mm wire — and the gauge is stated with each curve.')
