# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- Sec. 3.5 (#3, #4, #5, #7, #8)
E('The bamboo grain structure on which the benchmark\'s strain recovery depends is produced by the cyclic treatment described in Sec. 2.2, and both alloys were put through it. The outcome is the second, and independent, place at which the two alloys separate',
  'The bamboo grain structure on which the benchmark\'s strain recovery depends is produced by cyclic treatments of the kind described in Sec. 2.2, and both alloys were given such treatments on the schedules stated there: the LLM-alloy as ≈1–1.3 mm rod on the two- and three-cycle 1200 ↔ 900 °C schedules, and as 0.36 mm wire — together with the 0.64 mm benchmark wire — on the quartz-tube 1200 °C cycle. The cyclic treatments and the tests on the treated material were completed before the equilibrium calculations of Sec. 3.4 were made, so the treatment was chosen without knowledge that no single-phase α state was reachable in this alloy; that is a result of this study, not a premise of its design. It was applied because the comparison required the LLM-alloy to see the benchmark\'s full route, of which this treatment is the grain-structure step. Bamboo was not a requirement of the hypothesis (Sec. 1), which held that it would be unnecessary; with an α parent assumed, the treatment was expected to behave in this alloy as in the benchmark, so it was applied to see whether the alloy would develop bamboo under the benchmark\'s route, and what it did instead. The outcome is the second, and independent, place at which the two alloys separate')
