E('The benchmark is single-phase α at 1200 °C and so has nothing to pin a migrating boundary; the LLM-alloy is a two-phase α + γ mixture at that temperature, and — as Sec. 3.3 notes from the unchanged phase constitution of the diffraction pattern after the AGG treatment — that mixture is the equilibrium state rather than an incompletely transformed one. A boundary migrating through it must drag or bypass a second phase that no additional time at temperature will remove. The cyclic treatment works in the benchmark because repeated crossing of the γ ↔ α boundary leaves a small number of surviving α grains to grow without competition; in an alloy that is duplex on both sides of the cycle there is no such condition to exploit.',
  'The benchmark is single-phase α at 1200 °C and so has nothing to pin a migrating boundary; the LLM-alloy is calculated to be a two-phase α + γ mixture at that temperature in all three databases (**Table 3**), and the duplex constitution is observed after quenching from the 40-minute anneal (**Fig. 1c**) and from the cyclic treatment (**Fig. 8**, Sec. 3.3). A boundary migrating through it must drag or bypass a second phase that additional time at temperature will not remove, since the calculation places γ at 29–38% of the alloy in equilibrium at 1200 °C whatever its measured fraction after the quench (Sec. 4.1). The cyclic treatment works in the benchmark because repeated crossing of the γ ↔ α boundary leaves a small number of surviving α grains to grow without competition; in the LLM-alloy, calculated to be α + γ at 1200 °C and to enter single-phase α at no temperature below its melting point (Sec. 3.4), no leg of either cycle places the alloy in a single-phase α field — the 1200 ↔ 900 °C schedule runs between α + γ and the fully-γ field calculated for ≈850–1050 °C — so there is no such condition to exploit.')
E('Repeated cycling through the α + γ two-phase field creates numerous transformation interfaces,',
  'Repeated cycling into and out of the α + γ field creates numerous transformation interfaces,')
E('Holding ≈1 mm rod at 1200 °C for 40 minutes instead of 1 minute — the treatment whose microstructure is **Fig. 1c**, where grains exceed 100 µm — drops the elongation from 11.0% to 1.4%, at the same diameter and with the same 200 °C/3 h age applied to both. The specimen barely deforms before it breaks.',
  'Holding the 0.36 mm wire at 1200 °C for 40 minutes instead of 1 minute — the treatment whose microstructure is **Fig. 1c**, where grains exceed 100 µm — drops the elongation from 25.5% to 0.8%, with the same 200 °C/3 h age applied to both and on the same 127 mm gauge (one specimen each; **Fig. 10a** against **Fig. 2h**); after 80 minutes at 1200 °C it is 0.5% (**Fig. 10b**). The specimen fractures within its first loading segment, before the first unloading point of the protocol is reached.')
E('The AGG condition is mechanically poorer in exactly the same manner, which matters because it is the condition in which the benchmark is useful. The only replicated AGG condition is the 2-cycle AGG rod, whose two specimens failed at 568 and 608 MPa after 1.0% elongation — the 40-minute result again, reached by a different route. Boundary cracking is visible on the specimen surface, under stereomicroscope examination, after the three-cycle treatment followed by the 200 °C/3 h age. A single three-cycle-plus-age specimen reached 1006 MPa at 11.9% elongation, so the damage is not uniform, and no general claim about AGG-condition strength is made here on this evidence; what the record supports is that the treatment introduces boundary cracking and large specimen-to-specimen scatter without producing the grain structure it was applied to produce.',
  'The cyclically treated rod is mechanically poorer in the same manner, which matters because the coarsened state is the one in which the benchmark is useful. **Fig. 10** collects every tensile record of the LLM-alloy in the prolonged-anneal and cyclically treated conditions, plotted from the raw load–extension exports with the gauge of each test stated, since the gauges differ (Sec. 2.4). The two two-cycle rod specimens (≈1.3 mm, 127 mm gauge) failed at 568 and 608 MPa after 1.0% elongation, each surviving a single unloading–reloading cycle (**Fig. 10c**) — the 40-minute result again, reached by a different route. Of the two three-cycle-plus-age rod specimens, both recorded at 1.06 mm, the one tested on the 127 mm gauge failed at 478 MPa and 0.5% (**Fig. 10d**), while the one tested on a 6.0 mm gauge reached 1006 MPa and cycled to 11.9% (**Fig. 10e**); on a 6.0 mm gauge that elongation corresponds to about 0.7 mm of crosshead travel, much of it load-train and grip compliance, so the figure is not a demonstration of ductility and is not compared with the 127 mm elongations. Boundary cracking is visible on the specimen surface, under stereomicroscope examination, after the three-cycle treatment followed by the 200 °C/3 h age. No general claim about the strength of the coarsened condition is made on this evidence; what the record supports is that the treatment introduces boundary cracking and large specimen-to-specimen scatter in the rod without producing the grain structure it was applied to produce. The 0.36 mm wire of the quartz-tube cycle — the stock of **Fig. 1d**, which did not coarsen — behaves differently: it cycled to 8% and fractured at ≈26% on a 25.4 mm gauge, at 1020 MPa (**Fig. 10f**), so the loss of ductility follows the coarsening rather than the treatment as such.')
E('Cyclic loading–unloading–heating curves were not recorded in the AGG condition, and the reason is worth stating because it is not availability. In grain size that condition lies between the 1-minute and the 40-minute anneals at 1200 °C, both of which have been characterised: the former in **Fig. 2g** and **Fig. 3a**, the latter above. Neither shows any transformation, and in neither — as in every panel of **Fig. 2** — does an unloading segment depart from a straight line parallel to the initial elastic loading. A response bracketed by two curves that each show no transformation cannot itself show one; it can differ only in strength and in ductility, and the sign of both is already fixed by the elongation loss between the bounds.',
  'Two things the record does not contain should be stated. No loading–unloading–heating test on a cyclically treated specimen of either alloy is reported here; the two specimens of **Fig. 3** are the only ones for which that test is reported. The rod specimens fractured at 0.5–1.0% on the 127 mm gauge, below the 3% strain of the heating protocol, and the cyclically treated 0.36 mm wire of **Fig. 1d** was not given that test. What the cyclic records of **Fig. 10** do show is the unloading geometry of **Fig. 2**: every unloading segment that was recorded — one in each two-cycle rod, eight in the rod tested on the 6.0 mm gauge and eight in the quartz-tube wire — is straight, parallel to the preceding loading line, and returns no strain beyond σ/E. Nor were phase fractions refined for the 40-minute condition of **Fig. 1c**, which was examined optically only, or for the cyclically treated wire of **Fig. 8** (Sec. 3.3); the comparison of phase constitution between **Fig. 1a** and **Fig. 1c** is therefore qualitative — both are duplex — and is stated as such.\n\n![**Fig. 10.** Cyclic tensile records of the LLM-alloy in the prolonged-anneal and cyclically heat-treated conditions, plotted from the raw load–extension exports, with stress from the measured diameter and strain from crosshead extension over the gauge length of that test, which differs between panels and is stated in each: (a) 0.36 mm wire, 1200 °C/40 min followed by the 200 °C/3 h age; (b) 0.36 mm wire, 1200 °C/80 min; (c) two ≈1.3 mm rod specimens after the two-cycle 1200 ↔ 900 °C schedule of Sec. 2.2; (d) ≈1.06 mm rod after the three-cycle schedule and the 200 °C/3 h age, 127 mm gauge; (e) a second specimen of the same condition tested on a 6.0 mm gauge; (f) 0.36 mm wire after the quartz-tube 1200 °C cycle, 25.4 mm gauge, shown to fracture at ≈26%. Loading was in 1% increments per cycle as in Fig. 2. Strains on the 6.0 and 25.4 mm gauges are not comparable with those on the 127 mm gauge (Sec. 2.4).](figures/Figure_10.png)')
E('but an alloy that falls to 1.4% elongation at 100 µm grains cannot be carried to grains of 360 µm and remain a wire anyone can use.',
  'but an alloy that falls below 1% elongation at 100 µm grains (**Fig. 10a**) cannot be carried to grains of 360 µm and remain a wire anyone can use.')
E('In the LLM-alloy no such signal appears anywhere — not across the eight anneal conditions of **Table 2**, not with the 200 °C/3 h age reported to develop pseudo-elasticity in this family (**Fig. 2h**), and not after the AGG treatment.',
  'In the LLM-alloy no such signal appears in any condition reported here: not on unloading in the seven 1-minute anneals of **Fig. 2** (600–1200 °C), not with the 200 °C/3 h age reported to develop pseudo-elasticity in this family (**Fig. 2h**), not on heating in the one condition given the loading–unloading–heating test (**Fig. 3a**), and not on unloading in the cyclically treated conditions of **Fig. 10**. The cyclically treated condition has not been put through the heating test.')
E('The LLM-alloy, however, shows no transformation at either end of its own range — neither in the sub-20 µm condition nor after the 40-minute anneal that takes it past 100 µm (**Fig. 1c**), between which the AGG condition falls. A variable that is held at zero across a fivefold change in grain size is not the variable that grain size controls. Abnormal grain growth multiplies a transformation strain; it does not create one, and there is nothing here for it to multiply.',
  'The LLM-alloy, however, shows no transformation at the fine end of its own range (the sub-20 µm condition, **Figs. 2g and 3a**) nor in the coarsened rod that survived a loading cycle (**Fig. 10c, e**), and the 40-minute wire that takes it past 100 µm (**Fig. 1c**) fractures before an unloading segment can be recorded (**Fig. 10a**). A variable that is held at zero across the change in grain size the record covers is not the variable that grain size controls. Abnormal grain growth multiplies a transformation strain; it does not create one, and there is nothing here for it to multiply. Bamboo raises a recoverable strain that already exists; whether it could create one in an alloy that shows none is not tested here, because the LLM-alloy rod does not survive the treatment to the test.')

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- Sec. 4.1 (#5, #8)
E('The failure of the LLM-hypothesized alloy (50Fe-30Mn-12Al-4Ni-4Si at% with 1000 ppm C) to exhibit super-elasticity or a shape-memory response can be attributed to a fundamental misalignment between its phase stability and its active deformation mechanisms.',
  'Three levels of evidence run through this section, and they should be kept apart. What was measured is that, in each condition reported here — the seven 1-minute anneals of **Fig. 2** at 600, 700, 800, 900, 1000, 1100 and 1200 °C, the 1200 °C anneal followed by the 200 °C/3 h age (**Fig. 2h**), the loading–unloading–heating test at 1200 °C/1 min (**Fig. 3a**), and the cyclically treated conditions whose unloading segments were recorded (**Fig. 10c, e, f**) — the LLM-alloy recovered no strain beyond σ/E on unloading; that after the 1200 °C/1 min anneal it recovered none on heating (**Fig. 3a**), while the benchmark given the same anneal recovered ≈0.5% (**Fig. 3b**); and that the diffraction pattern of the 1200 °C/1 min wire was essentially unchanged by 10% strain (**Fig. 4a, b**). What is calculated is that no solution treatment within the processing window accessible here brings this composition to single-phase α, and that Sec. 3.4 finds no such field in the solid state at all; that statement reaches beyond the conditions reported to the composition itself, but it rests on equilibrium calculation in three databases rather than on measurement. What is not claimed is that the composition is incapable of super-elasticity under any processing: a solution or ageing treatment not tried here is untested (Sec. 4.4), and what this paper evaluates is the potential of this alloy as processed by the one route used here.\n\nThe absence of super-elasticity and of a shape-memory response in the LLM-hypothesized alloy (50Fe-30Mn-12Al-4Ni-4Si at% with 1000 ppm C), in the conditions just listed, is attributed to a misalignment between its phase stability and its active deformation mechanisms.')
E('it is that no accessible heat treatment produces that parent phase in the first place.',
  'it is that, on the calculation, no accessible heat treatment produces that parent phase in the first place.')
E('The duplex constitution that the calculation predicts at the solution-treatment temperature is precisely what is observed: the interlocked two-phase morphology of **Fig. 1b**, the Al-rich and Mn-rich EDS partitioning that accompanies it, and the coexisting α and γ reflections of **Figs. 4a and 5**. It also explains why the *constitution* is insensitive to annealing time even though the grain size is not: the duplex state survives 40 minutes at 1200 °C (**Fig. 1c**) and the full AGG cycle (Sec. 3.3) because it is the equilibrium state at that temperature, not an incompletely transformed one.',
  'The duplex constitution that the calculation predicts at the solution-treatment temperature is what is observed in kind, though not in proportion: the interlocked two-phase morphology of **Fig. 1b**, the Al-rich and Mn-rich EDS partitioning that accompanies it, and the coexisting α and γ reflections of **Figs. 4a and 5**. It also explains why the *constitution* is insensitive to annealing time even though the grain size is not: the duplex state is observed after 40 minutes at 1200 °C (**Fig. 1c**) and after the full cyclic treatment (Sec. 3.3) because both phases are calculated to be in equilibrium at that temperature, so neither is removed by longer holding; what the calculation does not reproduce is the measured proportion of the two, and that discrepancy is taken up next.')
E('One quantitative discrepancy should be stated plainly. The calculations place α in the majority at 1200 °C (62–71%), whereas the refinement of the quenched wire places γ in the majority (≈62%); the two agree that the alloy is duplex but not on which phase dominates. Because γ is the transformation product rather than the parent in this system, a partial α → γ transformation during cooling would invert the ratio, which would place the martensite start temperature above room temperature. That reading would supply a more complete account of the absence of super-elasticity than a driving-force argument alone — the transformation would already have run before the specimen was ever loaded, leaving nothing to induce — and it is what elevated carbon content would be expected to produce. The duplex morphology of Sec. 3.1 is consistent with this reading without establishing it, since a two-dimensional section does not reveal which constituent is continuous; the lath-like, transformation-refined appearance of the AGG-treated wire (**Fig. 1d**, Sec. 3.1) points the same way. We note it as the reading the present data favor rather than as an established result; direct calorimetric measurement of the transformation temperatures is required to confirm it, and is the most informative single experiment remaining on this alloy.',
  'One quantitative discrepancy should be stated plainly. The calculations place α in the majority at 1200 °C (62–71%, **Table 3**), whereas the refinement of the quenched wire places γ in the majority (≈62%); the two agree that the alloy is duplex but not on which phase dominates. The inversion is large — ≈62% γ refined against 29–38% calculated — and it is not peculiar to one pattern, since the pattern of the same wire after deformation is essentially unchanged in relative intensities (**Fig. 6a**). Two readings are open and the present data do not decide between them. The first is that the γ was present at 1200 °C in more than the calculated amount, either because the databases — which themselves span 62 to 71% α — overestimate α at that temperature, or because the wire, which enters the strand anneal cold-drawn ≈85% after its last process anneal at 1000 °C (Sec. 2.1), a temperature at which the primary database calculates it to be fully austenitic (**Fig. 9b**), was not carried to the 1200 °C equilibrium in the time available; phase fractions were not measured for the 40-minute condition (**Fig. 1c**) or for the cyclically treated condition (Sec. 3.3), so this sub-reading is untested here. The second is that part of the α present at temperature transformed to γ on cooling; since γ is the transformation product in this system (Sec. 3.3), a martensitic α → γ reaction during the quench would invert the ratio and would place the martensite start temperature above room temperature. The EDS partitioning of **Fig. 1b** does not discriminate between the readings. The Al-rich and Mn-rich constituents are the partitioning expected between α and γ at 1200 °C under either reading, and a martensitic γ formed from α on the quench would inherit that α\'s Al-rich composition [@bhadeshia2010steels] and so would not be separated from α by chemistry alone; the EDS result is reported qualitatively (Sec. 3.1), without a map of composition against crystal structure and without a fraction of either constituent. The microstructural test is a phase map with composition on the same area (EBSD with EDS), in which Al-rich regions indexing as FCC would be the signature of the second reading, or an areal fraction of the Mn-rich constituent on the same mount, which the first reading requires to match the ≈62% γ of the refinement and the second to fall below it; neither was performed. The lath-like appearance of the cyclically treated wire (**Fig. 1d**, Sec. 3.1) is consistent with either reading. Neither reading alters the conclusion of this section, which rests on the measured unloading and heating behaviour of Sec. 3.2 (**Figs. 2 and 3a**) and on the duplex constitution calculated for 1200 °C (Sec. 3.4) and observed after quenching from it (Secs. 3.1 and 3.3), not on when the γ formed. The direct test of whether a transformation occurs on cooling is differential scanning calorimetry of the strand-annealed wire through heating and cooling, with the benchmark wire as the control; it has not been performed, and the question is carried as open in Sec. 4.4.')
E('so there is no α matrix in which coherent B2 could form, and the microstructure the benchmark relies upon is unavailable to this composition at any temperature.',
  'so there is no α matrix in which coherent B2 could form, and the microstructure the benchmark relies upon is, on the equilibrium calculation of Sec. 3.4, unavailable to this composition at any temperature in the solid state.')

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- Sec. 4.4 (#1, #2, #7, #8)
E('The AI-guided alloy design did not achieve the intended super-elastic or shape-memory behavior because the model primarily relied on thermodynamic and literature-based correlations rather than a complete understanding of the complex phase-transformation mechanisms required for functional Fe-based SMAs. As shown in this study, the LLM-hypothesized alloy developed a stable multiphase microstructure consisting mainly of FCC γ and BCC α phases, and neither mechanical testing nor synchrotron diffraction provided evidence of stress-induced martensitic transformation; deformation was instead accommodated largely by conventional dislocation slip. Although the composition appeared reasonable from a thermodynamic and strengthening perspective, the AI model was unable to accurately predict the delicate balance among phase stability, transformation driving force, defect structure, precipitation behavior, and processing history that governs pseudo-elasticity. This outcome highlights a current limitation of AI-assisted alloy design: available models are often trained on composition–property relationships and equilibrium thermodynamics, while kinetic effects and microstructure evolution during manufacturing remain insufficiently represented.',
  'The workflow that produced this alloy should be stated before its outcome is interpreted, because the outcome belongs to that workflow and not to AI-assisted design in general. A deep-research agent was asked for lower-cost Fe-based compositions with the potential for super-elasticity; it returned six ranked candidate families, each with a stated rationale and a performance target (Sec. 2.1); one candidate was chosen and melted; and between the report and the melt no calculation of phase stability was made. As shown in this study, the LLM-hypothesized alloy developed a stable multiphase microstructure consisting mainly of FCC γ and BCC α phases, and neither mechanical testing nor synchrotron diffraction provided evidence of stress-induced martensitic transformation in the conditions reported here; deformation was instead accommodated largely by conventional dislocation slip. The composition appeared reasonable from a strengthening and literature perspective, and the rationale attached to it (Sec. 1) is coherent on its own terms. What that rationale did not contain, and what nothing later in the workflow supplied, was a check that the parent phase it specified could be produced by any treatment within the 1200 °C ceiling of Sec. 2.2. That is the limitation this study documents. It is a limitation of the workflow as it was run — a composition-level rationale carried to the furnace without a phase-stability and processing-window check — and not a demonstrated limit on what an LLM can propose, which one candidate cannot decide.')
E('the experimental claims of this paper are bounded accordingly. Three specific tests remain open, and are named here rather than left implicit.',
  'the experimental claims of this paper are bounded accordingly. A second session, given the processing ceiling and asked to screen its own candidates against it, was not run. It would answer a different question from the one this paper asks: a candidate that passes an equilibrium screen has met a necessary condition, not shown that it transforms — the D0₃ that formed here is not an equilibrium prediction either (Sec. 4.1) — so that question would be answered only once such a candidate is melted and tested, which is a further study. Three specific tests remain open, and are named here rather than left implicit.')
E('but each bounds a different part of the claim, and the paper does not assert what they would show.',
  'but each bounds a different part of the claim, and the paper does not assert what they would show. A fourth test bears on the interpretation of Sec. 4.1 rather than on the diagnosis: differential scanning calorimetry of the strand-annealed wire, with the benchmark as the control, would establish whether a transformation event occurs on heating or on cooling within the range scanned, which is the point on which the two readings of Sec. 4.1 differ; it would not by itself assign an event to α → γ or quantify the γ formed. It has not been performed. Likewise, the loading–unloading–heating protocol of **Fig. 3** has not been applied to the cyclically treated 0.36 mm wire (Sec. 3.5); the unloading records of **Fig. 10** bound what that test could show but do not replace it.')
E('It is also worth saying what a negative result of this kind is good for, since such results are rarely published in this area.',
  'What is claimed here can then be listed, together with what is not. First, this is, to our knowledge, the first LLM-hypothesized shape-memory composition carried physically from proposal through melting, processing and heat treatment to mechanical and synchrotron characterization; Sec. 1 gives the reason such tests are rare and published failures rarer. Second, the failure has a diagnosed mechanism with a control. At the solution-treatment temperature the measured chemistry is calculated to be duplex α + γ in three independent databases, the same chemistry with carbon removed is single-phase α in all three, and the benchmark\'s single-phase field is reproduced without fitting in the two databases able to carry it (Table 3, Fig. 9a); the duplex constitution the calculation predicts is the one observed in its phases, though not in its majority phase (Secs. 3.1, 3.3 and 4.1). The failure mode is therefore documented rather than surmised: a required phase field lying in the wrong place relative to the processing window, at a carbon content inside the range the agent\'s own hypothesis specified — 0.1–0.3 wt%, with the melt at the minimum (Sec. 2.1). Third, the record shows where the workflow stopped: the report recommended a CALPHAD check and the melt proceeded without one. What is not claimed as new is the check itself. Screening a candidate composition against its intended processing window by equilibrium calculation is an established step in conventional alloy design [@lukas2007calphad], and the report\'s own recommendation shows the agent treated it as one. The finding is that an LLM-assisted workflow omitted that established step, that the omission cost the heat and the work reported here, and that it was diagnosable before melting from the nominal composition and the processing ceiling alone (Sec. 3.4).\n\nIt is also worth saying what a negative result of this kind is good for, since such results are rarely published in this area.')
E('The present study should therefore be viewed not as a failure of AI itself, but as evidence that current AI models have not yet fully captured the complexity of structure–processing–property relationships in shape memory alloys.',
  'The present study should therefore be read not as a failure of AI itself, nor as a measure of what current models can or cannot capture, but as a documented instance of a design workflow that stopped one calculation short of the check its own output recommended.')

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- Sec. 5 (#1, #2, #7, #8)
E('validated against the benchmark alloy, identify the origin of this failure.',
  'validated against the benchmark alloy, indicate the origin of this failure.')
E('These results highlight an important limitation of current AI-assisted alloy design. While AI can rapidly generate compositionally plausible alloy candidates based on thermodynamic considerations and literature knowledge, it does not yet fully capture the complex interactions among composition, processing, microstructure evolution, and deformation mechanisms that govern shape-memory performance. They also point to a specific and inexpensive remedy: an equilibrium step diagram, checked for overlap between the phase fields the intended processing route requires and the temperatures at which that route can be run, would have rejected this composition before 2 kg of it was melted.',
  'The order in which this evidence arrived should be stated, because the interpretation changed with it while the observations did not. The mechanical and diffraction results above were obtained first, and they carry the same conclusion now as they did then: no stress-induced transformation in any of the conditions reported here, a duplex γ + α constitution unchanged by deformation, and a benchmark that transforms under the same protocol. The cause was at first attributed to carbon stabilising γ and lowering the driving force for transformation, on the strength of the two-heat comparison alone. That attribution could not be separated from the four other compositional differences between the heats (Sec. 4.2), and it is withdrawn. The calculations of Sec. 3.4, made afterwards and validated against the benchmark, replace it with a mechanism of a different kind: a single-phase α parent state is not produced at any temperature the wire could be annealed at, so the parent state the processing route requires was never reached; the α that is present (about 34%, Sec. 3.3) sits in a duplex mixture with no accessible field in which to age it (Sec. 4.1). Carbon remains the element implicated, but by the calculated closure of the single-phase α field at the solution-treatment temperature (Sec. 4.2) rather than by an inferred loss of driving force. What was reinterpreted is why the alloy does not transform; that it does not, and what it is instead, are the measurements the study began with.\n\nWhat these results show about the design workflow is specific. The agent produced a composition with a coherent rationale and a stated performance target, recommended that phase stability be checked by CALPHAD to guide experimental design (Sec. 4.4), and the melt proceeded without that check. The omitted calculation, run afterwards on the measured chemistry — and, in Sec. 3.4, on the nominal composition as well — would have rejected this composition before 2 kg of it was melted: at the 1200 °C ceiling of the process the alloy is calculated to be duplex rather than the single-phase α the route requires, in all three databases (Table 3). The limitation documented here is therefore one of the workflow as it was run — a composition-level rationale carried to the furnace without a processing-window check — and not a demonstrated limit on what an LLM can propose; one candidate cannot establish the latter, and this study does not claim it.')
E('Second, composition-only reasoning of the kind the agent performed is intrinsically blind to the distinction that decided this case — not whether a phase is stable, but whether the temperature at which it becomes stable can be reached in the equipment available; an LLM working from literature correlations has no reliable way to represent that constraint, and instructing it to consult thermodynamic data (Sec. 2.1) did not supply one. Third, whether a different processing window could unlock transformation in this composition is untested here, and casting the carbon-free variant identified in Sec. 4.2 remains the direct experiment that the in-silico control stands in for.',
  'Second, the reasoning that produced the composition stopped short of the distinction that decided this case — not whether a phase is stable, but whether the temperature at which it becomes stable can be reached in the equipment available. The report recommended the calculation that resolves that distinction and did not perform it (Sec. 4.4); whether the request put to the agent asked for such a calculation cannot be established, because the verbatim prompt was not preserved (Sec. 2.1); and whether a request that supplied the processing ceiling would have produced a candidate that both passes the screen and transforms was not tested here (Sec. 4.4). Third, whether a different processing window could unlock transformation in this composition is untested here, and casting the carbon-free variant identified in Sec. 4.2 remains the direct experiment that the in-silico control stands in for; the cyclically treated wire has not been given the loading–unloading–heating test, and the transformation temperatures of the alloy have not been measured (Sec. 4.4).')


def main():
    text = io.open(PATH, encoding='utf-8').read()
    bad = []
    for i, (old, new) in enumerate(EDITS):
        n = text.count(old)
        if n != 1:
            bad.append((i, n, old[:80]))
    if bad:
        for i, n, head in bad:
            print('EDIT %d matches %d times: %s...' % (i, n, head))
        sys.exit('no edits applied')
    for old, new in EDITS:
        text = text.replace(old, new, 1)
    io.open(PATH, 'w', encoding='utf-8', newline='\n').write(text)
    print('applied %d edits to %s' % (len(EDITS), PATH))


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
